    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104049",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$",
      "worked_solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and II only"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "I only",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "knowledge_point_source_id": 3569,
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-104049",
