    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104051",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980965/assets/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$",
      "worked_solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
